<p>Creativity doesn’t drive my blogging; software does. So <a href="https://daily.baty.net/">daily.baty.net</a> is back in rotation and is once again built using Tinderbox.</p>
<!-- more -->
<p><a href="https://eastgate.com/Tinderbox">Tinderbox</a> is special. I've been preaching this for more than 20 years. However, my use of Tinderbox has slowed down in recent years due to the distraction that is Linux. I felt I needed to move away from macOS-only software. I shouldn't have.</p>
<p>I've been &quot;going ham&quot; with Tinderbox recently, and it feels good. I used to <em>live</em> in its Map views, but that was back when I was working. Now, I use it mostly as a fancy outliner (it's a really good outliner) and as a blogging tool.</p>
<p>Tinderbox manages daily.baty.net. For a minute I was using Eleventy, but no longer. Tinderbox's export features are complex, and sometimes I get lost, but they're also very flexible and powerful.</p>
<p>Long story short, when I'm experiencing the rush that comes with rediscovering Tinderbox, I want to use it for everything, so I brought it back and I'm posting daily notes <a href="https://daily.baty.net/">there</a> instead of journal entries here.</p>
<p>In case you were wondering.</p>
